// examples/closure_cell.wll — closure cell-capture semantics (§3.3, §3.4)
// Demonstrates: a closure capturing a `LET MUT` cell and accumulating
// state across invocations.
//
// spec §3.3 (cell mutability flag is permanent): a binding created by
// `LET` (immutable) cannot be `SET` later. The legacy v0.4 form
//   LET(n, 0); LET(step, FUN((), LET(n, +(n, 1))));
// would `LET(n, +(n, 1))` shadow the outer `n` each call — the function
// returns NULL (per spec §3.1, `LET` is itself NULL-valued) and the
// shadowed inner `n` is discarded. To share mutation across closure
// invocations, the cell must be declared `LET MUT` and mutated via
// `SET` (spec §3.4).

LET MUT(n, 0);
LET(step, FUN((), (
    SET(n, +(n, 1));
    n
)));
LET(c, step()); LET(c, step()); LET(c, step());
PRINT(c);   // 3